# What never reaches the build context.
#
# Two jobs. The first is size and speed: the build context is uploaded to the
# daemon before anything runs, so `node_modules/` and `.venv/` alone are most
# of the wait for a build that will not use either.
#
# The second is the one that matters. `.env` holds real database credentials
# and the session signing secret. `COPY . .` is not used in this Dockerfile —
# it copies named directories precisely so a stray file cannot ride along — but
# a `.dockerignore` that omits `.env` is one careless `COPY` away from baking
# credentials into a layer, where they survive deletion in a later layer and
# are readable by anyone who pulls the image.

# ── secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# ── build artefacts that must be rebuilt, never copied ───────────────────────
# `client/dist` is produced by stage 1. Copying a local one in would ship
# whatever was last built on somebody's laptop, from a commit nobody recorded.
.venv/
venv/
client/node_modules/
client/dist/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# ── the project's memory, not its runtime ────────────────────────────────────
# These are why this repository is worth reading and none of them execute in a
# container. `docs/` is ~4MB of specification; `evidence/` holds screenshots.
.git/
.github/
docs/
# ...with ONE exception. `app/core/errors.py` and `app/modules/categories/
# service.py` read `contract.json` at RUNTIME, not at build time — the error
# code table and the seeded category list are loaded from the spec of record
# rather than transcribed into Python. Excluding it ships an image whose every
# error path raises FileNotFoundError, including the handler that reports
# errors. Found on the first real deploy, 2026-09-13; /healthz does not touch
# this code, so the CI smoke test passed against a broken image.
!docs/03 PRD/
!docs/03 PRD/api/
!docs/03 PRD/api/contract.json
# ...and a SECOND exception, found the same way and one layer deeper.
# `alembic/versions/0002_cr001_soft_delete_and_admin_daily.py:36` reads
# `security.sql` while MIGRATING, to avoid holding a second definition of a
# SECURITY DEFINER function. Migrations run from the entrypoint, inside the
# container, so this is a runtime dependency too.
#
# Production never noticed: its database was already at head, so alembic had
# nothing to apply and never opened the file. CI starts from an EMPTY database,
# runs every migration, and dies here. An image can therefore be "working in
# production" and unable to build a new environment from scratch — which is
# precisely what three environments needed it to do.
!docs/06 DataModel/
!docs/06 DataModel/security.sql
evidence/
goals/
handoffs/
tests/
workflows/
*.md
!client/public/fonts/README.md

# ── local tooling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/
tools/
alembic/versions/__pycache__/

# ── the nested worktrees ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `dev/` and `uat/` are checkouts of the other two branches sitting inside this
# folder. Without these two lines every `docker build .` from main uploads THREE
# copies of the project to the daemon as build context, and bakes two of them
# into the image.
dev/
uat/
